  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "When to stop repairing an air conditioner in Brampton",
   "html": "<p>Our rule for Brampton air conditioners is three strikes. A capacitor at year eight is strike one, a contactor at year ten is strike two, and a refrigerant leak at year twelve is strike three. By then you have paid for parts on a unit that is still running at its original efficiency, and the compressor is next.</p>"
           "<p>The exception is a house that has grown. If a basement suite was added, or two families now share the home, the original AC may have been fine for the house it was bought for and simply too small for the one it serves today. No repair fixes that. In those cases we size a new system for the full household and often split the load between a ducted heat pump upstairs and a ductless unit for the suite.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Capacitor, contactor, fan motor or drain on a unit under 10 years: repair</li><li>Refrigerant leak or coil on a unit past 12 years: compare both prices</li><li>Compressor failure on any unit past 10 years: replace</li><li>Any unit that was never sized for the current household: replace and resize</li></ul>"
           "<p>For the bigger picture on cooling and heating a shared Brampton house, see our <a href=\"/brampton/\">Brampton HVAC page</a>. For symptom-by-symptom help on any air conditioner in Ontario, the province-wide <a href=\"/ac/repair-maintenance/\">AC repair and maintenance</a> page goes deeper.</p>"},
